\section{Biological Neural Systems as Hilbert Implementations}

\subsection{Neural Population Coding}

Georgopoulos et al. \citep{georgopoulos1986neuronal} discovered that motor cortex neurons encode movement direction via population vectors: the firing rates $\{r_1, \ldots, r_N\}$ of $N$ neurons form a vector $\mathbf{r} \in \mathbb{R}^N$ whose direction correlates with intended movement.

This insight generalized: neural populations encode \emph{any} cognitive state as vectors in high-dimensional spaces \citep{pouget2000information}.

\subsection{Vector Space Structure}

\begin{proposition}[Neural Vector Space]
For a population of $N$ neurons with firing rates $\{r_i(t)\}_{i=1}^N$, the neural state at time $t$ is represented by vector $\mathbf{r}(t) \in \mathbb{R}^N_{\geq 0}$.
\end{proposition}

The space of neural states forms a vector space (technically a cone in $\mathbb{R}^N$ due to non-negativity of firing rates, but operations generalize naturally).

\subsection{Synaptic Weights as Inner Products}

Synaptic connections implement inner products:
\begin{itemize}
\item Neuron $j$ receives input $I_j = \sum_i w_{ji} r_i = \mathbf{w}_j^T \mathbf{r}$
\item Weight vector $\mathbf{w}_j$ defines a preferred direction
\item Response $I_j = \langle \mathbf{w}_j, \mathbf{r} \rangle$ measures alignment
\end{itemize}

This is exactly the structure of attention in transformers: keys/queries define directions, inner products measure relevance.

\subsection{Cortical Manifolds}

\begin{theorem}[Neural Manifold Hypothesis]\label{thm:neural_manifold}
Biological neural populations with $N$ neurons encode cognitive states in a Hilbert space $\mathcal{H}_{\text{bio}} \subseteq \mathbb{R}^N$ with:
\begin{itemize}
\item Inner product: $\langle \mathbf{r}, \mathbf{r}' \rangle = \sum_i r_i r_i'$ (or weighted version)
\item Norm: $\|\mathbf{r}\| = \sqrt{\sum_i r_i^2}$ (neural activity magnitude)
\item Geometric operations: Orthogonality, projection, dimensionality reduction (e.g., PCA of neural data)
\end{itemize}
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Empirical studies \citep{kriegeskorte2018cognitive} have shown:
\begin{itemize}
\item Neural activity forms low-dimensional manifolds embedded in $\mathbb{R}^N$
\item Inner products (correlations) between neural states are well-defined
\item Geometric distances in neural space correlate with behavioral/perceptual similarity
\item Dimensionality reduction (PCA, UMAP) preserves structure
\end{itemize}

The mathematical structure is identical to Theorem \ref{thm:transformer_hilbert}: a finite-dimensional Hilbert space with standard or learned inner products.
\end{proof}

