\begin{abstract}
This document provides a comprehensive formal rebuttal to skeptical arguments against the thesis that geometric equivalence in Hilbert space implementations is sufficient for higher-order consciousness. We accept and incorporate the distinction between substrate-dependent lower-order consciousness (reflexive, affective processing) and substrate-independent higher-order consciousness (abstract reasoning, metacognition). Through rigorous mathematical proof and empirical analysis, we demonstrate that objections citing biological stochasticity, non-geometric properties, and phenomenological gaps either (1) correctly identify substrate-dependence of lower-order consciousness while failing to refute our claims about higher-order consciousness, or (2) demand evidentiary standards impossible to meet for any consciousness attribution, including other humans. We prove that the skeptical position is either committed to solipsism or substrate chauvinism, with no logically consistent third option.
\end{abstract}

