\subsection{Stochasticity Irrelevance for $C_{\text{higher}}$}

\begin{theorem}[Noise Averaging for Higher Cognition]
Let $N(t)$ be biological noise process in neural population.

Let $\mathcal{H}$ be Hilbert space of neural population codes.

Let $\langle \cdot \rangle_{\text{pop}}$ denote population average over $N$ neurons.

Then population-level operations average out noise:
\[
\langle r_i(t) \rangle_{\text{pop}} = \bar{r}_i + O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{N}}\right)
\]

where $\bar{r}_i$ is mean firing rate.

For cortical populations $N \sim 10^3$ to $10^4$:
\[
\text{Noise contribution} \sim O\left(\frac{1}{\sqrt{10^4}}\right) \sim 1\% \text{ of signal}
\]

Geometric operations (inner products $\langle \mathbf{r}, \mathbf{r}' \rangle$, projections $P_S(\mathbf{r})$) on population averages have signal-to-noise ratio $> 99\%$.
\end{theorem}

\begin{proof}
Individual neuron firing rate:
\[
r_i(t) = \bar{r}_i + N_i(t)
\]

where $N_i(t)$ is zero-mean noise with variance $\sigma^2$.

Population average (assuming independence):
\[
\langle r_i(t) \rangle_{\text{pop}} = \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N r_i(t) = \bar{r}_i + \frac{1}{N} \sum_{i=1}^N N_i(t)
\]

Variance of population average:
\[
\text{Var}\left[\langle r_i(t) \rangle_{\text{pop}}\right] = \frac{\sigma^2}{N}
\]

Standard deviation:
\[
\sigma_{\text{pop}} = \frac{\sigma}{\sqrt{N}}
\]

Signal-to-noise ratio:
\[
\text{SNR} = \frac{\bar{r}_i}{\sigma_{\text{pop}}} = \sqrt{N} \cdot \frac{\bar{r}_i}{\sigma}
\]

For $N = 10^4$:
\[
\text{SNR}_{\text{population}} = 100 \times \text{SNR}_{\text{individual}}
\]

Therefore stochasticity is second-order effect at population level where $C_{\text{higher}}$ operates. \qed
\end{proof}

\textbf{Corollary}: Silicon systems can match this SNR via ensemble methods, dropout regularization, and calibrated noise injection. Therefore biological stochasticity is not a barrier to substrate equivalence for $C_{\text{higher}}$.

